// Module 2 quiz - Deep Learning for AI Startups
$CATEGORY: $course$/Module 2

::M2 Q1:: Why do networks need nonlinear activation functions? {
~To make training slower
~To reduce memory
~To normalize labels
=Without them, stacked layers behave like a single linear layer#Stacking linear layers without a nonlinearity is still one linear function, so the network could not learn curved boundaries like the spirals.
}

::M2 Q2:: Backpropagation is an application of which idea? {
=The chain rule#Backpropagation applies the chain rule layer by layer from the loss back to each weight.
~The central limit theorem
~Bayes' rule
~Matrix inversion
}

::M2 Q3:: What happens if the learning rate is far too large? {
~Training is always perfect
~The model never uses the GPU
=The loss can oscillate or diverge#Steps that are too big overshoot the minimum, so the loss bounces around or blows up.
~Gradients become zero by definition
}

::M2 Q4:: What is gradient checking used for? {
~Compressing models
=Verifying that hand-written gradients are correct#It compares your analytic gradients with numerical gradients or autograd to catch backprop bugs.
~Choosing the batch size
~Selecting features
}

::M2 Q5:: In gradient descent, weights move in which direction? {
~Along the gradient
~Randomly
~Toward zero always
=Against the gradient of the loss#The gradient points uphill, so stepping against it lowers the loss.
}
